# A device is shown by what its own files name (README: *Device icons*): the four files this program writes
# into a volume's root carry a long name that the file system there cannot hold, and the picture of the shape
# the icon was drawn for. The listing reads both back — and reads neither out of a file that carries no
# marker of ours.
# The stick of this run's own: its folder already holds those four files, written by this program's own writer
# before the scenario starts, pinned against a file system whose own label holds eleven characters.
fake-stick Samsung = $T/stick
fake-stick Broken = $T/broken
fake-stick Plain = $T/plain
action win.drives
# Where the volume names no folder — an unmounted stick has none — the machine's own answer stands: the label
# it carries and the themed icon of its kind, and no reading of a folder that is not there.
wait-drive Samsung
expect-drive-picture Samsung : none
# Mounting is what gives the volume a folder to read. From there the name pinned in the two text files is the
# name the volume is listed by, ahead of its label, and the picture the marked file names is what the row draws.
wait-side-drive Samsung : Not mounted — click to mount
side-drive-click Samsung : Not mounted — click to mount
wait-location $T/stick
action win.drives
wait-drive Samsung 64 GB Family Pictures
expect-drive-picture Samsung 64 GB Family Pictures : DevIcon.fil
# The side panel's own name for it is the same answer, because it is the volume's name and not a widget's.
wait-side-drive Samsung 64 GB Family Pictures : $T/stick
# The stick whose two picture files hold nonsense instead of an icon: it is still named by what its own files
# pin, and it draws no picture at all. The nonsense costs the picture and nothing else, and it is answered from
# what those files say about themselves — no reader is handed bytes that are no icon.
wait-side-drive Broken : Not mounted — click to mount
side-drive-click Broken : Not mounted — click to mount
wait-location $T/broken
action win.drives
wait-drive Broken Stick With A Rotten Picture
expect-drive-picture Broken Stick With A Rotten Picture : none
wait-side-drive Broken Stick With A Rotten Picture : $T/broken
# A stick of the same run whose folder holds nothing of ours keeps the name the machine gave it and the themed
# picture of its kind: nothing is read into it.
wait-drive Plain
expect-drive-picture Plain : none
# *Remove device icon* takes the four away through the row menu, and the listing answers the volume's own label
# and the themed icon again — and leaves the folder holding nothing of ours, the mark of ours on it included.
drives-menu Samsung 64 GB Family Pictures : Remove device icon
wait-dialog taken away from
dialog-button taken away from : Close
wait-drive Samsung
expect-drive-picture Samsung : none
wait-side-drive Samsung : $T/stick
# Taking an icon away works the same way where the pictures are nonsense: the names of ours go, the folder is
# left holding nothing of ours, and the volume reads as the label the machine carries.
drives-menu Broken Stick With A Rotten Picture : Remove device icon
wait-dialog taken away from
dialog-button taken away from : Close
wait-drive Broken
expect-drive-picture Broken : none
